1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
I start with a normal chat request. This is a real Qwen model running locally.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
The reply streams through the gateway. Its time and token count appear below.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Now I ask a follow-up, using the earlier conversation as context.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Both turns are complete. Next, let's send several requests together.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
I send twelve requests with round robin, which takes turns between the two workers.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Watch the slots fill and clear. Each worker has four active slots.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
I switch to least outstanding. It chooses using the number of waiting and running requests.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The same twelve-request group finishes again. We compare performance separately in repeated tests.

9
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:11,000
The gateway checks each client's limits before choosing a worker.

10
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:20,000
This burst exceeds the demo client's limit. Excess requests receive HTTP 429.

11
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Eight requests finish. Sixteen are rejected before reaching a worker.

12
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The response includes a retry hint, so clients can try later.

13
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Now I run a mixed workload and inspect an actual recorded model step.

14
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The blue piece generates one token. The amber piece processes a prompt.

15
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:57,000
The prompt uses seventy of the shared 128-token allowance. Decode tokens are counted separately.

16
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:06,000
QKV and MLP share computation, while Attention and KV data stay separate for each request.

17
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The service kept running while I inspected this step. All twenty-four requests have finished.

18
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Each worker has its own model copy and steps. These counters show its actual work.

19
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:35,000
I request a longer answer, then stop it while the model is still generating.

20
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
The partial answer stays visible, with an explicit cancelled outcome.

21
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Active slots and KV resources return to zero, ready for new requests.

22
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Finally, I stop the worker that is generating this answer.

23
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
The interrupted answer shows an error, without replaying partial text.

24
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
A new request completes on the surviving worker while the other stays offline.

25
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:18,000
I restart the stopped worker and wait for its model to reload.

26
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Now I send four new requests to check the restored service.

27
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
All four finish, using both workers. Capacity is available again.
